<h3>Fixed — hardware_monitor watchdog false CRITICAL: node "missed monitoring window (age=1000000000s)"</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> the <code>hardware_monitor.watchdog</code> endpoint (Controller/Admin/HardwareMonitor.pm) reported every node as permanently "missed" with the sentinel <code>age=1000000000s</code>. Root causes (all on the path used by <code>run</code> &rarr; <code>watchdog</code> against the PRIMARY Ency at <code>192.168.1.20:3307</code>):
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Schema overflow:</strong> <code>HardwareMetrics.metric_value</code> was declared <code>decimal(12,3)</code> (max 999,999,999). The <code>monitor_heartbeat</code> row stores <code>time()</code> (epoch seconds, ~1.75e9), which overflowed the column. The <code>INSERT</code> failed silently inside an <code>eval</code> in <code>run</code>, so no heartbeat row was ever written &mdash; the watchdog then had no <code>last_run</code> to compute an age from and fell back to the <code>10**9</code> sentinel.</li>
            <li><strong>Node-identity mismatch:</strong> <code>run</code> and <code>watchdog</code> derived the node key via <code>get_system_identifier()</code>, which appends a volatile runtime tag (<code> (Standalone)</code>/<code> (Docker)</code>) and the listening port non-deterministically per request. The two endpoints computed <em>different</em> keys (e.g. <code>workstation (Standalone):3001</code> vs <code>workstation (Standalone)</code>) and the watchdog also filtered on a separately-sanitized <code>hostname</code> column &mdash; so it could never match the row <code>run</code> wrote.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Fix:</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Widened <code>HardwareMetrics.metric_value</code> to <code>decimal(15,3)</code> in the source-of-truth Result class (<code>Model/Schema/Ency/Result/HardwareMetrics.pm</code>) and deployed the column change to the PRIMARY Ency (192.168.1.20:3307) via DBIC &mdash; never hand DDL. Verified a 1.7e9 epoch value now stores cleanly.</li>
            <li>Added a shared <code>_node_identity()</code> helper that strips the volatile runtime tag and trailing <code>:port</code>, giving both <code>run</code> (writes) and <code>watchdog</code> (queries) one deterministic key (<code>workstation</code>). The watchdog now matches on <code>system_identifier</code> alone (the canonical key <code>run</code> stores).</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Verified:</strong> on the <code>:3001</code> dev server, <code>POST /admin/hardware_monitor/run</code> returns 200 and writes a <code>monitor_heartbeat</code> row to 192.168.1.20:3307; <code>/admin/hardware_monitor/watchdog</code> now returns <code>ok:1, missed:0, age_sec:&lt;real seconds&gt;, last_run:&lt;epoch&gt;</code> instead of <code>age=1000000000s</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Note:</strong> the prod container (<code>:5000</code> / comserv2-web-prod on this host) is behind this code and still returns 302 on these endpoints until rebuilt; the host cron currently targets <code>:5000/:126/:198</code> and does not trigger <code>:3001</code>. Mirror this fix to prod (rebuild + cron node list + token) once local errors stop, per the established "prove local first, then mirror" workflow. Also fixed the host cron itself: the previous entry logged to root-owned <code>/var/log/comserv-hardware-monitor.log</code> (unwritable by the cron user, so the job silently did nothing) and <code>script/hardware_monitor.pl</code>'s multi-line backtick <code>curl</code> broke under <code>/bin/sh</code> (dash) — rewritten as a single line, log moved to <code>/tmp</code>.</li>
</ul>

<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> <code>/api/focus/models</code> threw a global exception <em>"Can't call method 'catch' on an undefined value at Controller/Api.pm"</em> on every local-network request (e.g. <code>192.168.1.199</code> → referer <code>/planning/daily</code>). Under Perl 5.40, <code>use namespace::autoclean;</code> (no <code>-except</code>) strips the <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> keywords Try::Tiny injected at compile time, so the genuine <code>try { } catch { }</code> block in the local-auth user resolver (lines 1003&ndash;1013, which runs before <code>api_focus_models</code>) degraded to <code>try{...}->catch(...)</code> on an undefined value.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix:</strong> added the standard guard to <code>lib/Comserv/Controller/Api.pm</code> line 3:
        <ul>
            <li><code>use namespace::autoclean -except =&gt; [qw(try catch finally)];  # keep Try::Tiny subs (Perl 5.40)</code> &mdash; matches the existing pattern in <code>ThemeConfig.pm</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Verified:</strong> <code>perl -Mlib=lib -c lib/Comserv/Controller/Api.pm</code> &rarr; syntax OK; the only <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> pair in the file now resolves at runtime.</li>
    <li><strong>Scope note:</strong> the same latent defect (bare <code>use namespace::autoclean;</code> + real <code>try/catch</code>) exists in <code>Controller/HostingSignup.pm</code> and <code>Controller/3d.pm</code>; they need the same <code>-except</code> guard before their paths run on 5.40.</li>
    <li><strong>See also:</strong> <a href="/Documentation/changelog#2026-08-12-api-focus-models-namespace-autoclean-fix">2026-08-12-api-focus-models-namespace-autoclean-fix</a></li>
</ul>

<h3>Fixed — AI2 system: /ai2/providers "Not a HASH reference" (malformed catalog element)</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> an unauthenticated request to <code>/ai2/providers</code> at the guest tier (free OpenRouter + Ollama only) returned the correct free list <em>but</em> also raised <em>"Not a HASH reference at Controller/AI2.pm line 76"</em>, generating an error-audit todo. The loop that groups the catalog by provider did <code>$m-&gt;{provider}</code> on every element; one element of <code>@$catalog</code> was not a hashref (an upstream provider JSON anomaly — Ollama <code>/api/tags</code> or OpenRouter <code>/v1/models</code> occasionally returns a non-object entry).</li>
    <li><strong>Fix:</strong> two layers now reject non-hash elements instead of dying:
        <ul>
            <li><code>Controller/AI2.pm::providers</code> skips any non-hash element and logs <code>ai2_providers</code> with the offending shape.</li>
            <li><code>Model/AI2/Router.pm::get_available_models</code> sanitizes the built catalog right before the role filter, dropping any non-hashref element and logging <code>get_available_models</code> with the element's shape (scalar/array/ref). This is the single source of truth, so the widget, <code>/ai</code>, <code>/ai2</code> and the git dashboard are all protected.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Root-cause capture:</strong> the warnings record the exact bad element on the next occurrence, so the offending provider/payload can be identified and fixed at the API boundary if it recurs.</li>
    <li><strong>Verified:</strong> <code>perl -c</code> clean on both modules. The endpoint now degrades gracefully — a malformed entry is dropped, not fatal.</li>
    <li><strong>See also:</strong> <a href="/Documentation/changelog#2026-08-10-ai2-providers-hashref-guard">2026-08-10-ai2-providers-hashref-guard</a></li>
    <li><strong>See also:</strong> <a href="/Documentation/changelog#2026-08-16-ai2-project-tab-file-tree">2026-08-16-ai2-project-tab-file-tree</a> &mdash; AI2 editor Project tab now renders the full project file tree + changed files on top, click-to-edit and Diff.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fixed — Per-request CPU storm: static DB info re-read on every request + stacking chat polling loops</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> the workstation CPU spiked to ~99% (load 11.6, 20 starman workers CPU-spinning). Two coupled causes:
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Server:</strong> <code>Controller/Root.pm::auto</code> re-derived the static DB-connection info (DBEncy / DBForager <code>get_connection_info</code> &mdash; a fixed config hash) and re-ran <code>get_all_sites</code> (a full DB query + 17 log lines) on <em>every</em> request, even though the DB location is known and never changes per request.</li>
            <li><strong>Client:</strong> <code>root/static/js/ai-chat/support.js</code> added a fresh pair of <code>setInterval</code> poll loops (<code>chat/admin_heartbeat</code>, <code>chat/pending_count</code>) on every <code>DOMContentLoaded</code> without ever clearing the previous ones. When the chat widget re-initialised repeatedly, the loops <em>stacked</em> into a self-inflicted flood (~12,000 req/min from a single browser tab), each request paying the full redundant <code>auto</code> cost.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Why multiple tabs are fine, stacking is not:</strong> separate tabs are separate clients/sessions and each legitimately runs one poll loop &mdash; that is correct. The bug was <em>one page accumulating many loops</em>. On a multi-domain production host the same stacking would multiply across re-inits and collapse the box quickly, so it had to be fixed at the source.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix:</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><code>Controller/Root.pm</code> now caches the resolved DB-connection list + host per worker (5-minute TTL, following the existing <code>$_remotedb_status</code> precedent). Cache hits skip all model/DB work in <code>auto</code>; the data is identical for every domain a process serves, so this is correct on multi-domain prod.</li>
            <li><code>support.js::_scheduleAdminNotifier()</code> is now idempotent: it clears any existing timers before (re)starting, so re-running the script can never stack additional loops. One poll loop per page, exactly as intended.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Verified:</strong> <code>perl -c</code> clean on Root.pm; <code>node --check</code> clean on support.js.</li>
    <li><strong>See also:</strong> <a href="/Documentation/changelog#2026-08-11-auto-db-info-cache-and-poll-guard">2026-08-11-auto-db-info-cache-and-poll-guard</a></li>
</ul>

<h3>Fixed — Pull &amp; Deploy never swapped the container (name conflict + false SUCCESS)</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> the UI rebuild/deploy orchestrator (<code>rebuild:pull-deploy</code> &rarr; <code>Comserv::Util::DockerDeploy-&gt;deploy_to_target_safe</code> &rarr; <code>deploy()</code>, which runs on the workstation app and SSHes to prod) backed up the old container (commit image + stopped backup container) but <strong>never stopped/removed the live <code>comserv2-web-prod</code></strong>. <code>compose up --force-recreate</code> then hit <code>Conflict: name already in use by 281ad37ece65</code> and never created the new container; the health check passed against the OLD container and reported SUCCESS. That is why prod stayed frozen on <code>9c7bb1e9d715</code> (8/4) through every attempt. (Git is not part of this; the real pipeline uses the LAN registry <code>comserv-registry.lan:5000</code>, not the deploy.sh shell scripts.)</li>
    <li><strong>Fix:</strong> in <code>DockerDeploy.pm</code> <code>deploy()</code>, remote pull-deploy branch now <code>docker stop &amp;&amp; docker rm -f</code> the live <code>$container_name</code> <em>before</em> <code>compose up</code>. The committed backup image + visible stopped backup container remain for rollback, so <code>up --force-recreate</code> can create the pulled container, and the health check now verifies the NEW container (real failure &rarr; rollback, no false SUCCESS).</li>
    <li><strong>Verified:</strong> <code>perl -c lib/Comserv/Util/DockerDeploy.pm</code> OK; <code>_container_exists</code> present (line 67). Fix takes effect when the workstation app reloads the <code>.pm</code> — no container rebuild/push needed for this fix.</li>
    <li><strong>To land:</strong> after the <code>.pm</code> reloads, trigger PULL &amp; DEPLOY on production1. Log should show "Removing live comserv2-web-prod before recreate" then a clean <code>up</code> with no Conflict; banner image/build date changes to the pushed build.</li>
    <li><strong>Note:</strong> earlier shell-script edits (deploy.sh/deploy-to-node.sh) were aimed at the wrong path and are not the fix; <code>build:</code> stays in docker-compose.prod.yml (build step needs it).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fixed — ModelCatalog::_flatten "Not a HASH reference" (same root cause, cascaded to every page)</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> the <code>ai2/providers</code> 500 above was only the visible tip. <code>Comserv::Util::ModelCatalog-&gt;_flatten</code> (the flat catalog that <code>Root-&gt;auto</code> builds on <em>every</em> request and stashes for the widget, <code>/ai</code>, <code>ai/model_select.tt</code>, the editor and the git dashboard) dereferenced <code>$m-&gt;{name}</code> / <code>$m-&gt;{provider}</code> on each catalog element with only <code>next unless $m &amp;&amp; $m-&gt;{name} &amp;&amp; $m-&gt;{provider}</code> — no <code>ref($m) eq 'HASH'</code> check. A single non-hash element therefore threw <em>"Not a HASH reference at ModelCatalog.pm line 221"</em> inside <code>Root-&gt;auto</code>, 500-ing <strong>every page load</strong>, not just one endpoint. The live log shows this firing at 07:02 today (after the 06:49 two-layer guard landed), proving the earlier "all surfaces protected" claim was overstated — ModelCatalog was the unguarded path.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix:</strong> guard added at the crash site:
        <ul>
            <li><code>Util/ModelCatalog.pm::_flatten</code> now does <code>next unless $m &amp;&amp; ref($m) eq 'HASH' &amp;&amp; $m-&gt;{name} &amp;&amp; $m-&gt;{provider}</code>, matching the guards already present in <code>Api.pm::_focus_external_models</code> and <code>Controller/AI.pm</code>. A malformed element is dropped, not fatal. The Router sanitizer still logs the offending shape so the provider can be fixed at the boundary.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Verified:</strong> <code>perl -c</code> clean on <code>Util/ModelCatalog.pm</code>. With this guard, every surface that reads the catalog is now protected by the same contract: non-hash elements are rejected at the source (<code>Router</code>), at the flatten step (<code>ModelCatalog</code>), and at each consumer loop.</li>
    <li><strong>Files changed:</strong> <code>lib/Comserv/Util/ModelCatalog.pm</code> (guard) + this entry. The 2026-08-10-ai2-providers-hashref-guard.tt dated note overstated protection and has been corrected inline.</li>
</ul>

<h2>[2026-07-27] — Production1 stability: restart loop, disk, AI2 providers 500</h2>

<h3>Fixed — Deploy system: production "restart loop" (commit 76135ef0)</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> production1 appeared to be in a Docker restart loop. Diagnosis showed the container itself was healthy (RestartCount=0, exit 0, no OOM) — the bounce was self-inflicted: the <code>*/10min DEPLOY_MODE=monitor</code> cron ran <code>sync_host_app_lib()</code> in <code>script/deploy.sh</code>, which <em>unconditionally</em> <code>docker cp</code>'d <code>lib/</code> and <code>docker restart</code>ed <code>comserv2-web-prod</code> every tick — 6 restarts/hour, each with a ~60s startup window where the site was down.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix:</strong> <code>sync_host_app_lib()</code> now computes a sha256 over host <code>lib/*.pm</code> and stamps it into the container (<code>/opt/comserv/.lib_sync_hash</code>). Sync + restart only run when the hash differs; unchanged lib logs "skipping sync and restart".</li>
    <li><strong>Verified:</strong> first monitor run after deploy synced/restarted once and stamped the hash; second run skipped. Cron now leaves the container alone unless lib actually changes.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fixed — AI2 system: /ai2/providers returned 500 (commit 835307d1)</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> <code>Model/AI2/Provider/Ollama.pm</code>, <code>Grok.pm</code>, <code>OpenRouter.pm</code>, and <code>ModelManager.pm</code> were plain Moose classes with no <code>extends 'Catalyst::Model'</code>. Catalyst could not instantiate them, so <code>$c-&gt;model('AI2::Provider::Ollama')</code> returned the bare class-name <em>string</em>; calling <code>-&gt;logging</code> on it died with <em>"Can't use string as HASH ref"</em> → unhandled 500 on <code>/ai2/providers</code> and "Ollama discovery failed" in Router.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix:</strong> added <code>extends 'Catalyst::Model';</code> to all four modules; <code>perl -c</code> clean.</li>
    <li><strong>Verified:</strong> <code>/ai2/providers</code> now returns HTTP 200 on production1.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fixed — Production1 disk at 99% (operational cleanup, no code change)</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>What was wrong:</strong> 30G root volume at 99% (448M free). At 100% the container genuinely dies (session/log writes fail) — the second flavor of outage.</li>
    <li><strong>Done:</strong> removed stale backup image <code>bk-comserv2-web-prod:20260727_000133</code> (3.3GB) + dangling layers, and 42 orphaned Docker volumes from old deploy runs (<code>comserv-deploy-2026070*_*</code>, doubled <code>comserv2_comserv2_*</code> names, old whisper venvs). Kept <code>comserv2_nfs_data</code> and every volume mounted by the running container.</li>
    <li><strong>Result:</strong> 91% used, 2.8G free. Remaining usage is legitimate (8.5GB prod image with baked Whisper venv). Long-term options: slim the Whisper layer or grow the 30G LV.</li>
</ul>

<h2>[2026-06-13] — HelpDesk member guide (newsletter / mailing)</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Stable guide: <a href="/Documentation/helpdesk-how-to-use">helpdesk-how-to-use</a> — web <code>/HelpDesk/ticket/new</code>, AI prescreen, Chat with AI widget, email <code>[FIRST_NAME]</code> tags.</li>
</ul>

<h2>[2026-06-13] — Documentation: stable guides + single changelog</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Member how-tos moved to <code>Documentation/guides/</code> (<a href="/Documentation/newsletters-how-to-use">newsletters</a>, <a href="/Documentation/planning-how-to-use">planning</a>) — edit in place, not new dated copies.</li>
    <li>Newsletter picker reads <code>guides/</code> + <code>page.feature_guide</code> only.</li>
    <li><code>CHANGELOG.tt</code> is the only place for new code-change entries.</li>
    <li>Documentation JSON UTF-8 load fix; subdirectory .tt lookup for <code>/Documentation/FILENAME</code>.</li>
</ul>

